Diameter of sphere $\mathrm{d}=2.4 \mathrm{ m}$ Radius, $\mathrm{r}=\frac{\mathrm{d}}{2}=1.2 \mathrm{ m}$
Surface charge density, $\sigma=80 \times 10^{-6} \mathrm{C} \mathrmm^{-2}$
Charge on the sphere, $\mathrm{Q}=\sigma \mathrm{A}
\begin{aligned}$& =$\sigma\left(4 \pi \mathrm{r}^2\right) \\ \text{$ and $} =80 \times10^{-6} \times3.14 \times(1.2)^2=1.45 \times10^{-3} \mathrm{C}
\end{aligned}$

Electric field, $\mathrm{E}=9 \times 10^4 \mathrm{ N} / \mathrm{C} 
Distance,  \mathrm{r}=$2 $\mathrm{ cm}=$0.02 $\mathrm{ m} For $infinite line change
 \begin{aligned} & E=$\frac{\lambda}{2 \pi \epsilon_0 r}=\frac{2 \lambda}{r}$ \times 9 \times 10^9 \\ & $\text{ Liner charges density, }$ \lambda=$\frac{E r}{2 \times 9 \times 10^9}$ \\ & =$\frac{9 \times 10^4 \times 0.02}{2 \times 9 \times 10^9}=$10^{-7} $\mathrm{Cm}^{-1}$\end{aligned}

Given that, radius of plate, $\begin{aligned}R \text{ and } =10 \mathrm{ cm}\\ \text{ and } =10 \times10^{-2} \mathrm{ m}
\end{aligned}$ Uniform electric field, $E=2 \sqrt{3} \times 10^5 \mathrm{NC}^{-1}$
Since, the angle between plate and electric field is $60^{{}^{\circ}}$.
